module sysl.container

/** A hash map, generic in its key and its value.
 *
 * The table is **one flat run of slots** and an entry lives inside it, so a map of a thousand keys
 * is one allocation rather than a thousand and one. That is the whole design decision, and it is
 * worth being exact about what it buys in a reference-counted language: a chained map holds each
 * entry through a `&Entry`, which is a heap cell with a count on it, so an insert allocates, a
 * removal releases, and a table that doubles walks every entry to relink it -- touching a count
 * each time. None of that happens here. `sizeof` says the same thing more plainly: a slot holding
 * two `u64`s is 24 bytes laid down in the table, while the pointer that would have replaced it is
 * 16 bytes *plus* the cell it points at.
 *
 * **A key is found by probing forward from where it hashes**, not by walking a chain: slots are
 * examined in order, wrapping at the end, until the key turns up or an empty slot proves it is not
 * there. Consecutive slots are consecutive memory, so a lookup that misses its first guess reads
 * the next one from the same cache line rather than following a pointer somewhere else.
 *
 * **A removed key leaves a tombstone rather than an empty slot**, because emptying it would break
 * every probe that had run past it on the way to something else. Tombstones accumulate, and the
 * answer is that they count against the table's load exactly as live entries do -- so a map that is
 * inserted into and removed from in equal measure rehashes periodically and stays fast, rather than
 * filling with holes that every lookup has to walk. `rehash` sizes from the **live** count, so that
 * same map settles at the size its contents deserve instead of doubling forever.
 *
 * **The stored hash is the third piece.** A slot keeps the `u64` its key mixed down to, which does
 * two things: a probe compares an integer before it compares a key, so a table of strings rejects a
 * wrong slot without touching either string; and `rehash` re-places every entry without hashing
 * anything, since the number it needs is already sitting beside the key.
 *
 * **An empty map has no table**, which is the fourth piece and the only one that is about what a map
 * costs when nothing is in it. `map()` allocates nothing; the first `put` makes the table. A program
 * that builds a map per loop iteration, or holds one in a struct that is usually empty, or makes one
 * on a branch that turns out not to be taken, pays nothing for any of them -- and none of those
 * callers could see that they were paying before, which is why the fix belongs here rather than at
 * each of them.
 *
 * ## What this replaced, and what that exercise found
 *
 * A chained map lived in `guide/hashmap` until this module existed, and it is gone -- a guide
 * program that teaches someone to write what they should have imported is the failure mode the
 * `guide/` sweep rule exists to catch. Its findings are the reason the language looks as it does
 * here, so they are carried rather than dropped:
 *
 * * **There was no `Hash` in the core catalog**, so a map had to declare its own trait and an
 * `impl` for every key type it wanted. Coherence made that permanent rather than untidy: two
 * libraries each declaring a `Hash` can never share a key type's implementation. `Hash` is a
 * catalog trait now (`sysl.hash`), which is what lets this module take `K: Hash + Eq` and ask
 * nothing else of anybody.
 * * **An enum has no zero value, so an array of them could not be declared without one.** The
 * repeat form `[None; n]` answered it by filling storage from a value rather than from a zero.
 * `[Slot.Empty; n]` here is that same form, and it is what makes the flat table *possible*: a
 * variant carrying no payload needs nothing of `K` or `V`, so it can fill a table of any key and
 * any value.
 * * **A generic container cannot make `[K; n]` storage**, because a bound may promise no value to
 * repeat and no trait sysl ships declares one. That finding is true and is often read wider than
 * it is -- it rules out a table of bare keys, and it does **not** rule out a flat table, because
 * the empty variant above sidesteps it entirely. The chained map read it as the wider claim and
 * paid a heap cell per entry for a limit that was not there.
 * * **Storage could not be asked for at a size worked out while running**, so the table was a
 * fixed directory of fixed blocks with a ceiling written into its type. Answered: an array form
 * written where a `[]T` is expected makes storage of its own, which is what `cell` is.
 * * **A bound is repeated at every declaration that needs it** -- `[K: Hash + Eq, V]` on the type
 * and again on each free function over it. There is no `where` clause and no name for a set of
 * bounds.
 * * **Two generics cannot solve each other.** `Map([Slot.Empty; n], 0, 0)` asks the constructor to
 * fix `Map`'s parameters from its arguments while the bare `Slot.Empty` waits to hear what it is
 * a slot *of*, and neither can go first -- so the binding in `map` below is annotated.
 * Inference is bidirectional but not simultaneous.
 * * **A bucket had to be named rather than re-indexed**, and a `ref` standing in `[]T` storage
 * kept that storage alive, so the table could not be grown while one was live. Nothing here
 * needs the rule: no reference into the table is ever taken, which is the same property that
 * makes `get` hand back a value rather than a pointer that a later `put` could strand.
 *
 * ## What it costs, and what was refused
 *
 * **A slot is as wide as a key plus a value plus a tag, whether or not anything is in it**, so an
 * empty table of large entries is bigger than an empty array of pointers would be. That is the
 * trade the flat layout makes and it is the right way round below a load factor of three quarters,
 * which is what `put` holds it to.
 *
 * **Robin Hood probing was refused.** It evens out probe distances and bounds the worst case, at the
 * cost of a displacement number in every slot and a shuffle on every insert and every removal. At
 * three quarters full, linear probing's average is short enough that the bookkeeping costs more
 * than it saves, and the simpler loop is the one a reader can check.
 *
 * **Backward-shift deletion was refused** for the same reason: it removes tombstones outright and
 * needs no periodic rehash, but the wrapping cases are exactly where a subtle bug would live, and
 * counting tombstones against the load gets the same guarantee out of code that is obviously right.
 *
 * **Nothing hands out a reference into the table** -- not `get`, not the cursor. A `&V` into storage
 * that `rehash` replaces would be a pointer at freed memory the moment the map grew, and the
 * chained map's own findings record having to reason about precisely that. The cost is that a value
 * is updated by putting it back rather than by being mutated where it lies.
 */

/** How many slots a fresh map has. A power of two, so an index is a mask rather than a division, and
 * doubling keeps every later count a power of two as well.
 */
private const initial_slots: usize = 8

/** The table is grown when three quarters of its slots are spoken for -- live entries and tombstones
 * alike, since a probe has to walk both. Written as a numerator over a denominator because there
 * are no fractions in an index calculation.
 */
private const load_num: usize = 3
private const load_den: usize = 4

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- storage

/** One slot of the table, which is one of three things: never used, used and vacated, or holding an
 * entry.
 *
 * **`Empty` and `Dead` are different and the difference is the whole probing scheme.** A probe stops
 * at `Empty`, because a key that hashed here would have been placed at the first free slot it found
 * and so cannot be further along. It does **not** stop at `Dead`: something was here when the key it
 * is looking for was inserted, so the chain it belongs to may continue past it.
 *
 * `Live` carries the hash beside the key so that a probe can reject a slot on an integer comparison
 * and `rehash` can re-place an entry without asking the key to mix itself down again.
 */
enum Slot[K, V]
    Empty
    Dead
    Live(hash: u64, key: K, value: V)

    /** What a slot holds, for a caller that has an index and wants the value at it. It answers an
     * `Option` rather than trapping on the empty cases because that is what its two callers want to
     * pass straight back out, and because a slot really can be empty -- there is no index into this
     * table that is guaranteed to hold something.
     */
    value(self) -> Option[V] = self match
        Live(_, _, v) -> Some(v)
        _ -> None

    is_live(self) -> bool = self match
        Live(_, _, _) -> true
        _ -> false
end Slot

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- map

/** A mapping from keys to values, with a key found in constant time on average.
 *
 * `dead` is carried rather than recomputed because it decides when to rehash and is asked for on
 * every insert; counting tombstones would be a walk of the whole table to answer a question about
 * one slot.
 */
struct Map[K: Hash + Eq, V]
    cell: []Slot[K, V]
    count: usize
    dead: usize

    len(self) -> usize = self.count

    is_empty(self) -> bool = self.count == 0

    /** How many slots the table has, which is what the load factor is measured against. It is not a
     * promise about how many entries fit -- the map rehashes at three quarters of this.
     *
     * **A map that has never been put into answers zero**, because it has no table: one is made by
     * the first insert. That is the one number here a caller might be surprised by, and it is the
     * whole of what "an empty map does not allocate" looks like from outside.
     */
    capacity(self) -> usize = self.cell.len

    /** The index a key's entry sits at, or `None`.
     *
     * The walk stops at `Empty` and steps over `Dead`, which is the rule the two variants exist to
     * express. It terminates because the load factor keeps at least one slot in four empty, so the
     * `Empty` case is always reachable from anywhere in the table.
     *
     * **A map with no table at all is answered before the probe starts.** A table is made by the
     * first `put`, so `cell` is empty until then, and `cell.len - 1` on an empty run is not a mask
     * of no bits -- it is every bit, and the index it makes is off the end.
     */
    private find(self, k: K) -> Option[usize]
        if self.cell.len == 0 then return None

        val h = k.hash()
        val mask = self.cell.len - 1
        var i = usize(h) & mask

        loop
            self.cell[i] match
                Slot.Empty -> break None
                Slot.Dead -> ()
                Slot.Live(sh, sk, _) -> if sh == h && sk == k then break Some(i)

            i = (i + 1) & mask
    end find

    get(self, k: K) -> Option[V]
        self.find(k) match
            None -> None
            Some(i) -> self.cell[i].value()

    /** The value a key names, or the one the caller supplies where it names none. The common shape
     * of reading a counter or an accumulator that may not have been started yet.
     */
    get_or(self, k: K, default: V) -> V = self.get(k).unwrap_or(default)

    has(self, k: K) -> bool = self.find(k).is_some()

    /** A key given a value: the entry it already had takes the new one, or a fresh entry is placed.
     *
     * **Room is made before the key is placed, not after**, because rehashing moves every entry and
     * an index worked out beforehand would name a slot in a table that no longer exists. The `+ 1`
     * is the entry about to be added: the test is whether the table would be over its load *after*
     * this insert, since discovering it afterwards would leave it over for the whole of the next
     * lookup.
     */
    put(*self, k: K, v: V)
        if (self.count + self.dead + 1) * load_den > self.cell.len * load_num then self.rehash()

        val h = k.hash()
        val mask = self.cell.len - 1
        var i = usize(h) & mask
        var vacant: Option[usize] = None

        loop
            self.cell[i] match
                // The end of the probe: the key is not in the table. It goes at the first tombstone
                // passed on the way here if there was one, which reclaims the slot, and at this
                // empty slot otherwise.
                Slot.Empty ->
                    vacant match
                        Some(d) ->
                            self.cell[d] = Slot.Live(h, k, v)
                            self.dead -= 1
                        None -> self.cell[i] = Slot.Live(h, k, v)

                    self.count += 1
                    break
                Slot.Dead -> if vacant.is_none() then vacant = Some(i)
                Slot.Live(sh, sk, _) ->
                    if sh == h && sk == k
                        self.cell[i] = Slot.Live(h, k, v)
                        break

            i = (i + 1) & mask
    end put

    /** A key taken out, answering whether it was there.
     *
     * The slot becomes a tombstone rather than empty, because a key that probed past it on the way
     * to its own slot must still be able to. The tombstone is counted, which is what eventually
     * triggers the rehash that clears it.
     */
    remove(*self, k: K) -> bool
        self.find(k) match
            None -> false
            Some(i) ->
                self.cell[i] = Slot.Dead
                self.count -= 1
                self.dead += 1
                true
    end remove

    /** Every entry dropped and the table let go entirely, so that a map used as scratch across a
     * loop does not keep the largest table any pass through it needed -- and a map that is cleared
     * and never used again costs nothing at all. The next `put` makes a fresh table, exactly as it
     * would for a map that had never held anything.
     */
    clear(*self)
        self.cell = []
        self.count = 0
        self.dead = 0

    /** A fresh table holding the same entries, sized from the **live** count.
     *
     * Sizing from the live count rather than doubling unconditionally is what makes tombstones a
     * self-correcting cost instead of a leak: a map that has had as much removed as added is over
     * its load and rehashes, and lands at the size its contents actually want -- possibly the size
     * it already was, with every tombstone gone. A map that is genuinely growing doubles, because
     * its live count is what put it over the line.
     *
     * Nothing is hashed here. Every entry carries the `u64` it mixed down to, and a table's index is
     * that number masked, so re-placing an entry is a mask and a walk.
     */
    private rehash(*self)
        var want = initial_slots

        while want < (self.count + 1) * 2
            want *= 2

        val old = self.cell

        self.cell = [Slot.Empty; want]
        self.dead = 0

        for i in 0..<old.len
            old[i] match
                Slot.Live(h, k, v) -> self.place(h, k, v)
                _ -> ()
    end rehash

    /** An entry put into a table that is known to have room and known not to hold its key: the two
     * things `rehash` can promise and `put` cannot. There is no tombstone to consider, because the
     * table it writes into was made empty a moment ago.
     */
    private place(*self, h: u64, k: K, v: V)
        val mask = self.cell.len - 1
        var i = usize(h) & mask

        while self.cell[i].is_live()
            i = (i + 1) & mask

        self.cell[i] = Slot.Live(h, k, v)
    end place

    /** A cursor over the entries, in no particular order -- the order is the table's, which changes
     * when it rehashes and is not something a program may rely on.
     *
     * **Do not modify the map while a cursor over it is live.** Doing so is memory-safe and nothing
     * more: the cursor holds the table as a view, so it keeps that storage alive and can never read
     * freed memory even when a `put` rehashes and hands the map different storage entirely. What it
     * *yields* in that case is unspecified. A `put` before the rehash writes into the very table the
     * cursor is walking and may be yielded; one after it goes somewhere the cursor cannot see and
     * will not be. Which side of the line a given `put` falls on depends on the load factor, so
     * there is no rule here worth learning -- collect what is wanted first, then modify.
     */
    walk(self) -> Cursor[K, V] = Cursor(self.cell, 0)
end Map

/** An empty map, which holds **no table at all** until something is put in it.
 *
 * **An empty map does not allocate**, which matters far more than it looks: a map built inside a
 * loop, held in a struct that is usually empty, or made and dropped on a branch that turns out not
 * to be taken costs a `malloc` and the `free` behind it every time, and no caller can see that it is
 * paying. The first `put` finds the table over its load -- nothing is over nothing -- and rehashes
 * to `initial_slots` before it places anything, which is the path `put` already took for a table
 * that had filled up. So there is no separate "grow from empty" case to get wrong.
 *
 * Reach for `map_with_capacity` where entries are known to be coming: a caller who names a size is
 * saying so, and gets its table immediately.
 *
 * **The annotation on `m` is required and is not noise.** `Map`'s parameters would have to come from
 * the constructor's arguments while the empty table waits to hear what it is a table of, and neither
 * can go first.
 */
map[K: Hash + Eq, V]() -> Map[K, V]
    var m: Map[K, V] = Map([], 0, 0)

    m

/** A map whose table is already big enough for `n` entries without rehashing, for a caller that knows
 * roughly how many are coming. The table is sized to twice `n` because the map rehashes at three
 * quarters full, so a table of exactly `n` slots would grow before the `n`th entry arrived.
 */
map_with_capacity[K: Hash + Eq, V](n: usize) -> Map[K, V]
    var want = initial_slots

    while want < n * 2
        want *= 2

    var m: Map[K, V] = Map([Slot.Empty; want], 0, 0)

    m
end map_with_capacity

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- walking

/** A position in a table: which slot to look at next.
 *
 * It takes **no bounds**, which is the point of writing them on the declarations that need them:
 * walking a table hashes nothing and compares nothing, so a cursor asks nothing of a key.
 */
struct Cursor[K, V]
    cell: []Slot[K, V]
    at: usize

/** A pair per entry rather than a reference to one, because there is no entry to refer to -- an entry
 * lives inside the table, and a reference into storage that `rehash` replaces is the one thing this
 * design exists to make impossible.
 */
impl[K, V] Iterate for Cursor[K, V]
    type Item = (K, V)
    next(*self) -> Option[(K, V)]
        while self.at < self.cell.len
            val s = self.cell[self.at]

            self.at += 1

            s match
                Slot.Live(_, k, v) -> return Some((k, v))
                _ -> ()

        None
    end next
